WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
- This is the key which opens the door

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.650
for future growth, and stability.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.240
- [Narrator] That's the CEO of Lucid,

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.100
a company you probably haven't heard of

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.850
even though it was valued
at $24 billion last year.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.860
- And the key he's talking about a SPAC

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.990
or a Special Purpose Acquisition Company.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.240
- [Narrator] From mid-2020 to 2021,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.940
SPACs were one of the hottest
investments on Wall Street

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.700
making up the majority of US IPOs.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.600
But by May of 2021, activity
had sharply declined.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.170
That month, there were
about 125 fewer IPOs

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.150
than there had been three months before.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.130
- It's really been stunning
how quickly it's come

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.864
to an end in the past year and a half.

00:00:36.864 --> 00:00:39.123
- [Narrator] So what went wrong?

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.410
A Special Purpose
Acquisition Company or SPAC

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.540
exists to merge with a private company

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.560
and take it public.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.470
It's an alternative to a traditional IPO.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:50.820
Here's how they work.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.430
First, founders raise money from investors

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.840
and set up a shell company,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.120
which they then take public with an IPO

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.280
or initial public offering.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.050
That's the SPAC.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.110
Then they search for a
private company to merge with.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.760
But there is a deadline.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.410
Usually two years.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.260
From here, there are two possibilities.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.300
If the SPAC doesn't find a company,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.240
it's liquidated, shareholders
receive their money back

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.220
and founders lose the millions it took

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.630
to set up a company.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.430
If the SPAC does find a target,

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.980
they merge taking that company public.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.320
The company replaces the
SPAC in the stock market

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.960
and investors can now buy shares.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.080
But why would companies want
to skip a traditional IPO?

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.820
- You can make business projections,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.260
you can communicate to the public

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.060
and do things that you normally can't

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.100
in a traditional initial public offering.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.660
- [Narrator] The method gained popularity

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.510
and in December 2020 and January 2021,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.480
many SPACs were raising
billions of dollars.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.280
They tapped into trendy business.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.750
- [Announcer] We're
going to space, Richard.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.040
- [Narrator] Like electric vehicles,

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.470
satellites and flying taxis.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.720
- And they were very attractive
to individual investors

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.450
who looked out and said, "I
want to own the next Tesla.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.830
I want to find these startups

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.330
that are gonna make me a lot of money."

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.620
- [Narrator] The buzz was also fueled

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.270
by social media star founders

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.280
who posted their accomplishments online.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.710
- So what happened is a
SPAC would announce a merger

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.050
and then creators like
Chamath Palihapitiya

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.690
or others would promote
the merger on CNBC.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:13.800
- It's a business that I think

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.690
will become extremely valuable.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:16.980
- They would tweet about it

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.410
and individual investors
would jump on the bandwagon

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.970
and these things would start trending.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.280
- [Narrator] The hype helped
fuel stock price gains

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.180
for companies like Lucid,
Virgin Galactic and Nikola.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.490
And SPAC founders
generated average returns

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:32.340
of more than eight times
their investment in 2020.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.110
- There was a period
in time that I tracked

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.200
where every single company
that announced a SPAC merger,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.730
shares of that SPAC went up.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.110
- [Narrator] But the flood of fundraising

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.150
and IPOing didn't last.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.230
After a record month

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:47.040
when SPACs raised $36
billion, things took a turn.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.110
- Even academics,
journalists, people like me

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.480
were writing articles saying
like this won't end well

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.010
because we have a lot of data showing

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.360
this historically never ends well

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.340
but people weren't really
listening in that moment.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.440
So it just became like a firestorm.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:59.580
- [Narrator] One of the two reasons

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.280
behind the fall was that a
few electric vehicle startups

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.800
had promised success in
their business projections,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.870
misleading their investors.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.540
Take Arrival, which sells electric buses.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.640
It said it would go from zero revenue

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.180
to $14 billion in just three years.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.160
Arrival's president
explained the projections

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.500
by saying that 2021 was a turning point

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.050
for rapid adoption of electric vehicles.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.720
- In many cases, what was under
the hood was not very pretty

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.540
and not as bold and like
spicy as people thought

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.010
at the time of the merger announcement.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.100
So there is a lot of bad
information frankly out there too

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.070
that was pushing people
to jump on the bandwagon.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.960
- [Narrator] This caught
the eye of regulators

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.480
and led to the second
reason behind the fall.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.090
In April, the SEC warned that some SPACs

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.640
might have to restate
their financial results.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.230
- Because at the end of the day,

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.080
we need to ensure that
investors are protected.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.216
- [Narrator] Both reasons
spooked investors.

00:03:51.216 --> 00:03:52.980
Share prices declined

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.870
and many investors withdrew their money

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.910
rather than hold on to shares of startups

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:59.250
after they completed SPAC deals.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.960
After the summer of 2021,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.670
companies started to think twice

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.410
before going public with a SPAC.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.420
- A lot of companies are
really second guessing

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.310
whether they want to
do SPAC deals as well.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.500
So that kind of positive effect

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.750
that made SPACs boom is
now working in reverse

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.190
where now the negative connotation

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.080
is making everyone second guess things.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:16.913
- [Narrator] Activities stayed

00:04:16.913 --> 00:04:20.010
at a relatively low consistent
rate for the next year.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:24.300
Then in March 2022, the SEC
proposed a set of regulations

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.650
that would make it harder for SPACs

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.980
to raise money from investors
and execute mergers.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.930
- Ive' asked staff for recommendations

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.300
about how we might update our rules

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.370
so that investors are better informed

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.210
about the fees, the costs,
the conflicts that may exist

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.350
with these SPACs.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.970
- [Narrator] The goal of the regulations?

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:44.070
To make companies disclose information

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.020
more like a traditional IPO.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.640
- But a lot of the damage
had already been done

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:48.473
by that point.

00:04:48.473 --> 00:04:50.490
That sentiment though that regulators

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.530
are stepping in and going to
regulate these things more,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.300
that's also had a chilling effect for sure

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:55.440
in the SPAC market.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.720
- [Narrator] By May, about 90% of SPACs

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.520
that merged during the 2020 boom

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.160
were trading below their
initial listing price.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.140
Analysts estimate that if the current pace

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.480
of mergers continues, a large percentage

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.090
of the roughly 280 SPACs
that have deadlines

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:12.330
in the first quarter of
2023 won't find a match.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.030
Recently, SPAC activity is at a low.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.850
- SPAC activity has basically
drawn to a standstill.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.710
In July, no new SPACs raised money.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.140
Early last year, there were basically tens

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.280
of new SPACs every week.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.380
The market, along with
the broader stock market

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.270
and market for newly public companies

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.280
has definitely reversed a lot.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.260
- [Narrator] After interest
rates climbed in the spring,

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.570
speculative investments are losing favor

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:34.980
and valuations for companies

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.330
that went public through SPACs

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:40.029
may continue to come down
from the highs of 2021.

00:05:40.029 --> 00:05:42.612
(upbeat music)

